    "### Q7: How Computers See Images\n",
    "To represent a color photo in matrix form, we can expand our 2D grayscale matrix into a 3D tensor with dimensions height × width × 3. Each pixel would no longer be a single grayscale value from 0 to 255, but a triplet of values corresponding to the RGB (Red, Green, Blue) channels, where each channel ranges from 0 to 255 (e.g., [255, 0, 0] for pure red). To convert this image into tabular data suitable for machine learning algorithms like tabular classifiers:\n",
    "\n",
    "We flatten the 3D tensor into a 1D row vector by concatenating the RGB values for every single pixel in the image (resulting in width × height × 3 features per image).\n",
    "We then append this vector as a single row into a larger dataset matrix, where each column represents a specific color channel at a specific pixel coordinate, and the final column stores the target label (for example: \"cat\", \"dog\", etc.).\n"
   ]
